From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 21:30:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] test(model): exhaustive decision-matrix checker for jams
 (J1/J2/J3)

A Deno test (tests/model/decision-matrix_test.ts, helpers under
tests/model/) that checks whether a Sentinel record can jam: J1 an
absorbing, non-terminal state nothing announces as needing a person; J2 a
cycle no ranking bounds; J3 a per-pass cap or ordering that starves an
eligible record.

Part A (census): 76 blocker classes x shapes x head CI, each run through
the REAL maintenance pass (runHostedAutonomy over in-memory state and a
fake repository surface) with the base tip moved, base CI green/red, at
+0h/+25h/+9d/+31d: 159 verdicts, 2352 real passes. Absorbing = nothing
moves it once the tip moved; visible = every pass names it.
Part B (lifecycle): runtime control flow modeled per code path (each
anchored to its source text), every decision taken by the real pure
policy (reviewRoundsLeft, ciFixesLeft, decideCiGate, attribution,
routes, ceilingParkMessage, isWaiting, observeGatewayVpsDelivery);
parks link back through the census. 5704 states and 9972 edges from
every admissible initial state; termination by lexicographic ranking
over SCCs under cooperative (strongly fair), adversarial and
owner-driven worlds. A step pure code cannot decide takes the REAL
repair loop's verdict: a review request whose admission settled
ambiguous and whose submission never landed is deferred without a write
on every run, past the review bound ("review intent has no exact bound
journal" precedes the expiry recovery), while a landed one moves; live
on ai.ubq.fi 604, 630 and 647.
Part C (fairness): fleets larger than each cap through the real passes
and the real repair loop (delivery-queue rig).
Part D (known positives, caught on pre-fix code): re-retiring every pass
(verbatim 01f482529^ planner, pinned to the current one minus the fix),
the base_moved merge loop (pre-180769ef4 reconcile), and the
output-bound overrun (pre-63143fb92 producer filter; the real
outputBoundCells classifies the live 1127 receipt).
Part E (PR continuity): the lifecycle carries the record's own pull
request (none/open/closed). The real advanceToCorrection keeps
target.pr, markTerminalBlocked clears it while the real GitHub port
reuses the exact own open PR on the deterministic branch, and only a PR
closed unmerged (retireClosedPublication) leads to a new one. Every way
from review or delivery back to a publication (P0/P1 correction, CI-red
correction, red head before a refresh, the refresh itself) resumes the
open PR; the only route to a new PR is the owner closing it, the J2
owner-close loop. Mutating either fact makes the test fail.

An absorbing state the owner rules require to progress (REVIEWED and
BASE_MOVED ceiling parks on a moved base, a legacy record red before
review, a merge receipt that no longer parses) is a jam even though the
maintenance log names it: the line reports a status, not that a person
is needed (J1:logged). Owner gates announced every pass are reported as
terminal by design.

KNOWN_JAMS lists the 73 counterexamples on the current code (launcher
3f041b46d, runtime R80) and KNOWN_PR_REPLACEMENTS the one route to a new
PR; the test asserts the checker finds exactly those, so a fix must
update them. No runtime or launcher code is changed. Whole run about
1.5 s.

